# Result: Sector Condition Stability

The framework's first major *Lyapunov result*, stated as a specific instantiation of a more general sector-persistence template ( #result-sector-persistence-template) whose abstract form lives in the appendices. The mismatch dynamics are taken in their general nonlinear form: mismatch changes at rate (correction function applied to mismatch) plus (environmental disturbance). The correction function is required only to satisfy the **local sector condition** — that its inner product with the mismatch is at least $\alpha$ times the mismatch's squared magnitude, within a radius-$R$ region where it remains valid. The sector condition is what generalizes the linear ODE: it captures the qualitative essence of correction (the function points inward with at least baseline efficiency) without committing to a specific functional form like linear, saturating, sigmoid, threshold, or PID. Saturation, thresholding, and basin boundaries all live under one Lyapunov argument.

Under this sector condition plus bounded disturbance, the chapter's headline persistence inequality is *derived*: $\alpha \gt \rho/R$ guarantees the agent persists. When the inequality holds, the mismatch is ultimately bounded by $R^\ast = \rho/\alpha$, and the "adaptive reserve" — the additional disturbance the agent can absorb before mismatch reaches the edge of the valid region — is $\alpha R - \rho$. The threshold is *tight*: over the class of correctors carrying sector floor $\alpha$ it is necessary as well (no weaker condition can deliver the guarantee), and for a corrector whose sector bound is radially tight — the linear case — it is a genuine agent-level if-and-only-if. For a general fixed corrector, failure of the inequality means the containment *guarantee* is lost, not that escape is certified ( #deriv-sector-condition Lemma A.1N).

Under **Model S** (stochastic disturbance), the analog is sharper and qualitatively different: the steady-state root-mean-square mismatch scales as $\sigma_w\sqrt{n/(2\alpha)}$ — the square root of the disturbance-to-correction ratio. Model D scales as $1/\alpha$, Model S as $1/\sqrt{\alpha}$: **correction is less effective against noise than against drift**. The scaling separates two genuinely different physics of adaptation under deterministic vs stochastic environments. Both scalings are the lag term of a lag/noise trade-off: the correction is driven by the observed innovation, so a stronger correction also injects more channel noise. Past the optimal gain, faster correction on the same observations enlarges the error instead of shrinking it (Widrow et al. 1976; #deriv-sector-condition, Setup).

The linear ODE from Chapter 3 ( #hyp-mismatch-dynamics) is recovered as the special case where the sector condition holds globally with $\alpha = \mathcal{T}$. The general sector-condition framework proves the persistence threshold is not an artifact of the linear approximation: every bounded-correction system carries such a threshold, and $\alpha \gt \rho/R$ is the sharpest condition derivable from the sector floor alone ( #deriv-sector-condition Lemma A.1N). The result is also where the structural-adaptation-necessity result will be anchored: when disturbance exceeds the model class's capacity (i.e., $\rho/\alpha \gt R$), the sector condition fails. This *is* the dynamical trigger for needing a new model class with larger valid radius or better efficiency — which #result-structural-adaptation-necessity treats formally a few segments later.

## Formal Expression

This segment is the **single-agent epistemic instantiation** of the sector-persistence template ( #result-sector-persistence-template). The template's state variable is $\xi = \delta(t) \in \mathbb{R}^n$, the estimation error $\bar\delta = \bar o - \hat o$ of #def-mismatch-signal (not the observed innovation, which carries channel noise no correction removes); the correction function is $F(\mathcal{T}, \delta)$; the disturbance $w(t)$ is environmental change plus the channel noise the correction feeds back ( #deriv-sector-condition, Setup); the region of validity $R$ is the model class capacity.

*[Formulation]*

$$\frac{d\delta}{dt} = -F(\mathcal{T}, \delta) + w(t)$$

*[Assumption (sector-condition)]*

$F$ satisfies the local sector condition (template condition (T2)) for $\lVert\delta\rVert \leq R$:

$$\delta^T F(\mathcal{T}, \delta) \geq \alpha \lVert\delta\rVert^2$$

with $\alpha \gt 0$. Here $\alpha$ is a *correction rate* (units $t^{-1}$, like $\mathcal{T}$): the correction function $F$ is the event-rate-aggregated, continuous-time field, so the sector bound it satisfies is the per-time rate. Disturbance is bounded: $\lVert w(t)\rVert \leq \rho$ (Model D, GA-2) or white with per-coordinate intensity $\sigma_w^2$, $d\delta = -F\,dt + \sigma_w\,dW_t$ (Model S, GA-2S). Grounding of (T2) for gain-based agents: #der-gain-sector-bridge gives the per-time rate $\alpha = \nu \cdot \eta^\ast \cdot c_{\min}$ (the event rate $\nu$ promoting the per-event efficiency $\eta^\ast c_{\min}$ to the rate this inequality consumes). The linear case $F = \mathcal{T} \cdot \delta$ — itself the per-time correction field — yields $\alpha = \mathcal{T}$ exactly ($\nu\eta^\ast$ at $c_{\min} = 1$).

*[Derived (from sector-persistence-template)]*

The template's Model D conclusion specializes to: $\delta(t)$ is ultimately bounded by $R^\ast = \rho/\alpha$, and the agent is guaranteed to persist when

$$\alpha \gt \frac{\rho}{R}.$$

The condition is sufficient for the individual agent and tight at class level — necessary for the uniform guarantee over all correctors with sector floor $\alpha$, and agent-level necessary exactly when the sector bound is radially tight (the linear case); see #deriv-sector-condition Prop A.1 and Lemma A.1N. The adaptive reserve is $\Delta\rho^\ast = \alpha R - \rho$ — the additional disturbance the agent can absorb before $R^\ast$ exceeds the valid region.

The template's Model S conclusion specializes to: the steady-state RMS mismatch is $R^\ast_S = \sigma_w\sqrt{n/(2\alpha)}$ (where $n = \dim(\delta)$), and mean-square persistence requires $\alpha \gt n\sigma_w^2/(2R^2)$. Model D scales as $1/\alpha$; Model S scales as $1/\sqrt{\alpha}$ — correction is less effective against noise than against drift. These scalings hold at fixed $\rho$ and $\sigma_w$. Because the disturbance includes channel noise injected in proportion to the gain, they describe the lag part of the bound. For linear correction the full Model S steady state is $n(\sigma_{\text{env}}^2 + \alpha^2\sigma_\nu^2)/(2\alpha)$, which is smallest at the Kalman gain and grows above it ( #deriv-sector-condition, Setup).

Full Lyapunov proofs: #deriv-sector-condition Props A.1, A.1S, A.2.

## Epistemic Status

*Exact.* Both results are direct instances of the sector-persistence template applied to the single-agent epistemic case. Template precondition (T1) is satisfied because no correction should be applied at zero mismatch; (T2) reduces to the local sector condition above and is grounded structurally by #der-gain-sector-bridge for gain-based agents; (T3) is the disturbance-model choice (D or S), a domain question. The linear ODE of #hyp-mismatch-dynamics is the special case where (T2) holds globally with $\alpha = \mathcal{T}$ — the per-time identity, both sides being event-rate-weighted correction rates (units $t^{-1}$); the sector framework generalizes this to saturating, thresholded, and structurally-limited correction functions under the same persistence condition. Disturbance-model choice is a domain question, not a theory question.

## Claim Record

### For a sector corrector under bounded disturbance, a correction rate above the disturbance over the region certifies persistence, with ultimate bound and reserve given in closed form

**Register:** formal — the template's Model D conclusion specialized to $\xi = \bar\delta$ (Formal Expression, *[Derived (from sector-persistence-template)]*; proofs #deriv-sector-condition Prop A.1, Lemma A.1N, Prop A.2).

**Population:** $\dot\delta = -F(\mathcal T, \delta) + w$ with $\delta$ the estimation error, (T1) and the sector condition on $\lVert\delta\rVert \leq R$ with floor $\alpha$, $\lVert w(t)\rVert \leq \rho$ (the total disturbance), started inside the region. Sufficient for each corrector; necessary over the class with floor $\alpha$, and agent-level only under radial tightness. For gain-based agents with directional fidelity $\alpha = \nu\eta^\ast c_{\min}$ (#der-gain-sector-bridge); for a linear corrector $\alpha = \mathcal T$ at the optimal gain.

**True of:** the linear corrector under constant disturbance $\rho \lt \alpha R$: the error settles at $\rho/\alpha$, and the reserve $\alpha R - \rho$ is exactly the shock that puts it on the boundary.

**False of:** the dip corrector of #deriv-sector-condition (Remark after Lemma A.1N), which fails the condition tenfold and stays contained, so failure of the inequality is loss of the guarantee, not certified escape, for a general corrector (as the opening says).

**Falsifier:** a corrector in the population with $\alpha R \gt \rho$ whose error leaves the region; it would refute "the agent is guaranteed to persist when $\alpha \gt \rho/R$".

**Instantiated:** not applicable.

### At fixed disturbance the certified error falls as one over the correction rate against bounded drift and as one over its square root against white noise

**Register:** formal — the Model S specialization and the scaling comparison (Formal Expression, last paragraph; #deriv-sector-condition Prop A.1S).

**Population:** the population above under GA-2S (additive isotropic white noise of per-coordinate intensity $\sigma_w$), at fixed $\rho$ and $\sigma_w$. With channel noise injected through the gain, $\sigma_w$ depends on $\alpha$ and the scalings describe the lag term only; for linear correction the full steady state is $n(\sigma_{\text{env}}^2 + \alpha^2\sigma_\nu^2)/(2\alpha)$. Under the sector condition on the ball only, what is bounded is the error counted while still inside the region.

**True of:** the Ornstein–Uhlenbeck corrector: stationary RMS $\sigma_w\sqrt{n/(2\alpha)}$, so doubling $\alpha$ divides it by $\sqrt 2$, against division by $2$ for $\rho/\alpha$.

**False of:** a gain raised past the Kalman gain on a fixed noisy channel, where the error grows with $\alpha$ (#deriv-sector-condition, Setup).

**Falsifier:** at fixed $\sigma_w$, a sector corrector whose killed second moment exceeds $n\sigma_w^2/(2\alpha)$ in the limit; it refutes the Model S specialization.

**Instantiated:** not applicable.

### Crossing the persistence threshold is the trigger for structural adaptation and calls for a new model class

**Register:** normative — a design claim about the remedy, tested by comparing remedies on agents past the threshold. The formal content beneath it, that a corrector with a finite region has a certificate threshold, is the first entry. Sources: Discussion, "Generalizing the persistence threshold" ("proves the persistence threshold … is a structural necessity of any bounded-correction system") and "Connection to structural adaptation" ("When $\rho/\alpha \gt R$ … The sector condition fails — this is the dynamical trigger for structural adaptation …, requiring a new model class with larger valid radius $R'$ or better efficiency $\alpha'$"); the opening states both.

**Population:** correctors with a finite region $R$ under Model D. What the derivation supplies is a threshold for the *certificate*: sufficient for each corrector, necessary at class level (#deriv-sector-condition Lemma A.1N).

**True of:** a radially tight corrector whose model class admits no larger $\alpha R$: past the threshold the outward disturbance forces exit, and only a class with larger $R$ or $\alpha$ restores a certificate.

**False of:** the reading of "structural necessity" as necessity for a given agent (the dip corrector stays contained below threshold). The sentence "the sector condition fails" when $\rho/\alpha \gt R$: the sector condition is a property of $F$ on $\mathcal B_R$ and does not depend on $\rho$; what fails is the certificate. "Requiring a new model class": a linear corrector on a noisy channel whose gain lies above its Model S window is restored by lowering the gain, and an infeasible window by better observations (#result-persistence-condition), neither a new model class.

**Falsifier:** for the normative half, a structurally failing agent restored within its model class by retuning its gain or by better observations; the window of #result-persistence-condition supplies one, so the "requiring" sentence is refuted as stated.

**Instantiated:** no.

## Discussion

**Why the sector condition.** The linear ODE assumes correction scales linearly with mismatch forever. Real adaptive systems saturate, exhibit thresholding, or break down when the model class is exhausted. The sector condition captures the minimal structural requirement: the correction must point in the right direction with at least baseline efficiency $\alpha$.

**Generalizing the persistence threshold.** In the linear case, $\alpha = \mathcal{T}$ (adaptive tempo). The general result $\alpha \gt \rho/R$ proves the persistence threshold ( #result-persistence-condition) is a structural necessity of any bounded-correction system, not an artifact of the linear approximation. This result addresses *structural persistence* — the machinery's capacity to bound mismatch — not operational persistence (current proximity to $R$) or continuity persistence (identity through time). See Persistence in `LEXICON.md` for the full disambiguation.

**Two kinds of guarantee, not only two rates.** The Model D and Model S bounds differ in kind as well as scale: under bounded disturbance with $\alpha R \gt \rho$ the persistence region is positively invariant ($P(\tau_R \lt \infty) = 0$), while under additive stochastic forcing exit from any bounded region is certain over an unbounded horizon ($P(\tau_R \lt \infty) = 1$) for every corrector satisfying the sector condition and every $\alpha$ — #deriv-sector-condition Corollary A.1S.1. Model S persistence is a mean-square / finite-time guarantee, never pathwise-forever; $\alpha$ sets the typical scale, the fixed-time tail, and the expected time before exit, which grows like $e^{\kappa_R}$ with $\kappa_R = \alpha R^2/\sigma_w^2$ (#deriv-sector-condition Prop A.1S-τ), not whether exit eventually occurs. At the mean-square threshold $\kappa_R = n/2$ the linear benchmark leaves within about one correction time; the condition certifies scale, and margin in $\kappa_R$ buys time. On a fixed noisy channel the time a stronger correction can buy is bounded: the exponent is capped at the Kalman gain, the stay peaks at a gain below it and falls past it, and only better information, a larger region or a slower world raises the bound.

**Connection to structural adaptation.** When $\rho/\alpha \gt R$, disturbance exceeds the model class's capacity. The sector condition fails — this is the dynamical trigger for structural adaptation ( #result-structural-adaptation-necessity), requiring a new model class with larger valid radius $R'$ or better efficiency $\alpha'$.
